#[summary("범위 for의 틀린 프로그램: 끝을 포함하는 범위의 색인, 불변식, 탈출 뒤의 사실 (backlog 2K-01d)")]
mod rf {
    // `..=` reaches `a.len()`.
    #[effects(pure)]
    fn past_end(a: &[i64]) -> i64 {
        let mut s: i64 = 0;
        for i in 0..=a.len() {
            s = a[i];
        }
        s
    }

    // The invariant breaks at the step: `c` grows by two.
    #[effects(pure)]
    fn twice(n: u64) -> u64
        requires few(n),
    {
        let mut c: u64 = 0;
        for i in 0..n
            invariant c == i,
        {
            c = c + 2;
        }
        c
    }

    spec fn few(n: u64) -> bool {
        n < 1000
    }

    // A range that is not empty leaves `s` at 0.
    #[effects(pure)]
    fn maybe_empty(lo: u64, hi: u64) -> u64 {
        let mut s: u64 = 1;
        for i in lo..hi {
            s = 0;
        }
        10 / s
    }

    // `continue` steps the variable, so the invariant must hold after it.
    #[effects(pure)]
    #[uses(few)]
    fn skip(n: u64) -> u64
        requires few(n),
    {
        let mut c: u64 = 0;
        for i in 0..n
            invariant c == i,
        {
            if i % 2 == 0 {
                continue;
            }
            c = c + 1;
        }
        c
    }

    // `break` keeps the state of its iteration: `last` is 3 there.
    #[effects(pure)]
    #[uses(few)]
    fn stop_early(n: u64) -> u64
        requires few(n),
    {
        let mut last: u64 = n;
        for i in 0..n {
            if i == 3 {
                last = i;
                break;
            }
        }
        10 / (last - 3)
    }

    // A decreasing variable is not a `for`: `n - i - 1` underflows at `i == n`.
    #[effects(pure)]
    fn reverse(n: u64) -> u64 {
        let mut s: u64 = 0;
        for i in 0..=n {
            s = n - i - 1;
        }
        s
    }
}
